  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 1246 AM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nFriday-Sunday...A longwave trough over the West Coast will lift\na weak trough in the subtropical jet northward into the \nSouthern Plains on Friday. Pressure falls across the Central \nPlains and southwesterly flow aloft is expected to transition \nthe boundary over central Missouri into a warm front. Isentropic\nupglide within the 295-305 K layer is likely to support \nadditional showers and storms (50-70% coverage) Friday into \nSaturday. As the subtropical trough approaches Saturday night, \nanother round of scattered showers (20-40% coverage) could form \nand persist into Sunday morning. By Sunday afternoon, shortwave \nridging sets back in, providing the area a brief period of \ndryness before heading into a more active pattern early next \nweek. By the end of the weekend, there is decent chance (60-90%)\nfor most of the CWA to see rainfall amounts ranging between \n0.25-0.75 of an inch.\n\nMonday-Tuesday...The longwave trough over the western CONUS \nfinally ejects eastward into the Rockies as surface cyclogenesis\noccurs again across the south-central Plains on Monday. Beyond \nthis point, there are two possible scenarios: (1) the \nsubtropical jet becomes out of phase with the polar jet leading \na more southern track or (2) the jets stay in phase and the low \nmoves into the Mid-Mississippi River Valley. This will have \nmajor implications on our precipitation amounts, temperatures, \nand winds early next week. The only certainty among the \nensembles is the precipitation type being rain as temperatures \nare likely (70-90%) to stay above 40 degrees through the middle \nof next week. In terms of rainfall amounts, latest NBM guidance \nhas a 50-70% of 1+ inches of rainfall between Monday and \nWednesday morning, but again, these probabilities are \nsusceptible to shift as confidence improves on the track of the \nsystem.",
